privacy-first RAG application that enables users to chat with local documents without uploading them to the cloud.

The application has two main pipelines: indexing and querying.

During indexing, the user selects a folder containing documents such as PDFs, DOCX files, or text files. 
FolderMind extracts the text, splits it into overlapping chunks, and generates embeddings for each chunk 
using Ollama's nomic-embed-text model. 
Each chunk, along with its embedding and metadata, is stored in ChromaDB.

During querying, the user's question is also converted into an embedding using the same embedding model. 
ChromaDB performs semantic similarity search to retrieve the most relevant chunks. 
Those chunks are combined with the user's question to build a RAG prompt, 
which is sent to the LLM (llama3.2 running on Ollama) to generate the final answer.

We chose a RAG architecture because LLMs don't have access to a user's private documents. 
RAG augments the LLM with relevant external context, resulting in accurate, grounded answers while keeping all data local.
